`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"One Llama 3 configuration frames the lecture",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The card is one row of the Llama 3 report: the dense 405B model on 16,384 GPUs at 8K context ",e.jsx(a,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),". Read it with the table of the previous slide. Its TP, CP, PP, DP and sharding fields each name one of its rows, so the run splits the batch, the matrices, the layers and the training state. Context parallelism is 1, so at this length the sequence is not split. The model is dense, so it has no experts to split. MFU is Week 4's model FLOPs utilization, which the Checkpointing & MFU tab extends to many GPUs."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The degrees multiply to the number of GPUs, because every GPU holds one combination of a TP, CP, PP and DP position: ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\mathrm{TP} \\times \\mathrm{CP} \\times \\mathrm{PP} \\times \\mathrm{DP} = 8 \\times 1 \\times 16 \\times 128 = 16{,}384",display:!1,trail:"."})]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The exercise applies the same reasoning at a small scale. You measure DDP and ZeRO-1 on a few CPU processes, then predict what four H100s would hold."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"This week adds distributed.py",reading:!0,accent:6,number:"7",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
