`]}),e.jsx(w,{view:"ranks"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Ranks, world size and process groups",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Distributed training runs one process per GPU, and every process runs its own copy of the same training script. From here on, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," counts the ranks that take part in a collective, all the ranks of the run in the DDP and ZeRO examples, not parameters as in Week 2's ",e.jsx(s,{math:"6NT",display:!1,trail:"."})]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The figure's two nodes are servers with eight GPUs each, so the world size is 16. Rank 9 runs on the second GPU of Node 1. Its local rank is 1, the same as rank 1's on Node 0."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A run can hold several process groups at once, and each communication step runs within one of them. The highlight buttons outline the two group shapes that tensor and data parallelism use. A tensor-parallel group exchanges partial results in every layer, so it stays on the fast links inside one node, like ranks 0 to 7. When tensor parallelism fills each node, a data-parallel group takes the GPU with the same local rank on every node, like ranks 1 and 9. The traffic of such a group crosses the network between the nodes."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The launcher ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"torchrun"})," starts one process per GPU with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"--nproc-per-node=gpu"}),". Among the environment variables it sets for each process, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"RANK"})," holds its rank, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"LOCAL_RANK"})," its local rank and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"WORLD_SIZE"})," the number of processes in the job ",e.jsx(a,{id:"pytorch-torchrun",children:"PyTorch Contributors, 2026"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Collectives move and combine data",accent:0,number:"9",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
