`]}),e.jsx(s,{math:"\\text{bytes per GPU} = 2(N-1)\\,\\frac{S}{N} = 2S\\,\\frac{N-1}{N}",label:"(1)"}),e.jsx(x,{compact:!0,initialOp:"all-reduce"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The ring sends 2S(N − 1)/N bytes per GPU",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ring is the standard algorithm for an all-reduce. In data parallelism, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S",display:!1})," is the gradient buffer, the parameter count times the bytes per gradient. For the 7B model with bf16 gradients, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S = 7 \\times 10^9 \\times 2\\,\\text{B} = 14\\,\\text{GB}",display:!1,trail:"."})]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Arrange the ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," GPUs in a ring: each sends to the next GPU and receives from the previous one, and the last GPU sends to the first. Because every step moves one chunk of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S/N",display:!1})," bytes per GPU, counting the steps is enough to count the bytes."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The first phase is a reduce-scatter in ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N-1",display:!1})," steps. In each step, every GPU sends one chunk to the next GPU, which adds it to its own copy of that chunk. In the following step, a GPU passes on the chunk it has just added to. Each partial sum therefore gains one GPU's contribution per step as it travels around the ring. After ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N-1",display:!1})," steps, every chunk has collected all ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," contributions, and each GPU holds one chunk that is the full sum over all GPUs."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The second phase is an all-gather in another ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N-1",display:!1})," steps. Each GPU passes its fully reduced chunk to the next GPU, which keeps a copy and passes it on in the following step. After ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N-1",display:!1})," steps, every GPU holds all ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," reduced chunks, which together form the full sum."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Formula (1) multiplies the ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2(N-1)",display:!1})," steps of the two phases by the ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S/N",display:!1})," bytes of each ",e.jsx(a,{id:"patarasuk2009ring",children:"Patarasuk & Yuan, 2009"}),". It is a derived count under the ring model and leaves out latency and overlap with computation. Dividing these bytes by a measured time gives the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"bus bandwidth"}),", the bytes each GPU's link carried per second."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The factor ",e.jsx(s,{math:"(N-1)/N",display:!1})," is below 1 for every ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," and approaches 1 as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," grows. So each GPU always sends less than ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2S",display:!1,trail:","})," and adding GPUs hardly changes what it sends. Patarasuk and Yuan show that in any all-reduce some GPU must send at least this much. The ring, in which every GPU sends exactly this much, is therefore bandwidth-optimal ",e.jsx(a,{id:"patarasuk2009ring",children:"Patarasuk & Yuan, 2009"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Take ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N = 4",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S = 1\\,\\text{GB}",display:!1,trail:"."})," Each chunk holds 0.25 GB, and the ring has six steps. Each GPU sends 0.75 GB in each phase, 1.5 GB in all, the figure in the explorer's readout. In the explorer, a cell labelled ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k/4"})," holds a partial sum of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"k",display:!1})," of the four GPUs' contributions, and a cell labelled ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"c0"})," to ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"c3"})," holds a chunk summed over all four."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["NCCL does not always run a ring. Every step also pays a fixed startup cost, its latency, whatever the size of the message. The ring pays it in each of its ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2(N-1)",display:!1})," steps. For small tensors this latency can dominate, and a tree algorithm, which needs fewer rounds, can be faster. For large buffers the sustained bandwidth matters more. NCCL chooses among its algorithms by message size and topology."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"An all-reduce is a reduce-scatter followed by an all-gather"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ring's two phases show that an all-reduce is a reduce-scatter followed by an all-gather. After the reduce-scatter, each rank holds one distinct reduced shard of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S/N",display:!1})," bytes. The all-gather only copies these shards, so every rank ends with the same full sum that an all-reduce produces. The two halves can run together or apart. DDP runs them together on the gradients, as one all-reduce. ZeRO separates them so that the training state stays sharded. The ZeRO & FSDP tab returns to the identity to price ZeRO's communication."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"NVLink is about nine times faster than the network",accent:3,number:"12",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
