`]}),e.jsx(w,{view:"links"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A link's bandwidth sets a floor under communication time",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Where the bytes travel decides whether a split is affordable. Inside a node, H100 GPUs communicate over NVLink 4. Its 450 GB/s per GPU counts one direction, half of the 900 GB/s that NVIDIA reports for both directions together ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia2022hopper",children:"Andersch et al., 2022"}),". Between nodes, traffic crosses InfiniBand NDR at 50 GB/s per link and direction ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia2026dgxh100",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". The other link inside a node, PCIe Gen5 x16, carries 64 GB/s raw in one direction ",e.jsx(a,{id:"pcisig2019pcie5",children:"PCI-SIG, 2019"}),". With all three rates counted the same way, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"450/50 = 9",display:!1,trail:":"})," NVLink is roughly an order of magnitude faster per GPU than the network between nodes ",e.jsx(a,{id:"ultrascale2025",children:"Tazi et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The figure gives every GPU its own network card, as in an NVIDIA DGX H100 node, so the legend counts one InfiniBand link per GPU ",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia2026dgxh100",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". The 50 GB/s per GPU is therefore an illustrative share of the fabric. The figure leaves out how the switches between the nodes are shared when many GPUs send at once."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Week 4's roofline bounded a kernel's time by ",e.jsx(s,{math:"T \\ge \\max(F/P_{\\mathrm{peak}}, B/\\beta)",display:!1,trail:","})," where ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B/\\beta",display:!1})," is the time to move ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B",display:!1})," bytes across a memory tier of bandwidth ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\beta",display:!1,trail:","})," such as HBM. A link between GPUs obeys the same kind of bound: a GPU that sends ",e.jsx(s,{math:"V",display:!1})," bytes over a link of bandwidth ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\beta_{\\mathrm{link}}",display:!1})," needs at least ",e.jsx(s,{math:"t_{\\min} = V/\\beta_{\\mathrm{link}}",display:!1,trail:"."})," For the ring's ",e.jsx(s,{math:"V = 1.5\\,\\text{GB}",display:!1,trail:","})," the slide's two times are these bounds. Over PCIe, the bound is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"1.5\\,\\text{GB}/(64\\,\\text{GB/s}) = 23.44\\,\\text{ms}",display:!1,trail:"."})]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A measured collective takes longer than these bounds. How much longer depends on its route, contention, per-message latency and overlap with computation."})]})]})]}),`
