`]}),e.jsx(k,{view:"amortize"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Bigger batches amortize a fixed communication bill",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Each DDP step all-reduces the gradients once, and formula (1) of the ring all-reduce gives the price. With ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S",display:!1})," the size of the gradient buffer in bytes, each of the ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," ranks sends ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2S(N-1)/N",display:!1,trail:"."})," Here ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S",display:!1})," is the parameter count times the bytes per gradient, so the batch does not enter it. For the 7B model with 2-byte gradients, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S = 14\\,\\text{GB}",display:!1,trail:"."})," At ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N = 4",display:!1,trail:","})," each GPU then sends ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2 \\times 14\\,\\text{GB} \\times 3/4 = 21\\,\\text{GB}",display:!1})," per step."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The compute of a step does grow with the batch. With ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b",display:!1})," the batch on each rank, a larger ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b",display:!1})," adds forward and backward work behind the same bytes on the wire. Compare the two rows of the timeline: the all-reduce bar keeps its length, while four times the batch makes the forward and backward bars four times longer. The all-reduce's share of the step falls from 30% to 11%."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The batch cannot grow usefully without limit. Week 5 introduced the critical batch size. Below it, a larger batch cuts the number of optimization steps needed to reach a training target. Past it, the extra examples in each step no longer pay for themselves, so a larger batch stops being a cheap way to amortize communication."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["There are two ways to add ranks. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Strong scaling"})," fixes the logical global batch and divides it across more ranks, so ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b",display:!1})," falls as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," grows. It asks whether the same step finishes faster. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Weak scaling"})," fixes ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b",display:!1,trail:","})," so the global batch ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N \\times b",display:!1})," grows with ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1,trail:"."})," It asks whether throughput grows while each rank keeps the same work, and it changes the optimization, which the critical batch size limits."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Buckets let communication overlap backward",accent:7,number:"17",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Backward computes the last layer's gradients first"}),`
