`]}),e.jsx(k,{view:"buckets"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Buckets overlap the all-reduce with the backward pass",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A naive DDP waits for the whole backward pass and then all-reduces every gradient. While it communicates, the GPU computes nothing, and while the GPU computes, the link idles. Production DDP overlaps the two. It groups the gradients into buckets of a fixed size, the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"bucket_size"})," of your ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"DistributedConfig"}),". A backward hook marks each gradient as ready once it is computed, and a bucket's all-reduce launches when all of its gradients are ready."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"In each row of the timeline, the upper lane is computation and the lower lane communication. In the upper row, one all-reduce of all gradients starts when the backward pass ends, and the update waits for it. The lower row uses four buckets, one per layer. The last layer's bucket travels while the layer before it computes its gradients, and each further bucket starts as soon as its layer is done. Only the first layer's bucket is left after the backward pass, so the step ends at 7.25 units instead of 9.5. Both rows send the same bytes, so bucketing hides communication time without reducing it."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Gradient accumulation changes when the synchronization happens. Inside a region such as PyTorch DDP's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"no_sync()"})," context, each rank runs several local microbatches and accumulates their gradients into ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:".grad"})," without launching an all-reduce. The first backward pass outside the region synchronizes the accumulated gradients before the optimizer step. If each of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," ranks accumulates ",e.jsx(s,{math:"a",display:!1})," microbatches of size ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b",display:!1,trail:","})," a step covers ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N \\times a \\times b",display:!1})," examples. Keeping the old global batch means dividing ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b",display:!1})," by ",e.jsx(s,{math:"a",display:!1,trail:"."})," When to synchronize and how large the batch is are two separate choices."]})]})]})]}),`
