`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Each layer is gathered, used and released",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["PyTorch implements ZeRO-3, which shards the weights too, as fully sharded data parallel, FSDP2. Outside each layer's computation, the parameters, gradients and optimizer state stay sharded, each rank holding one ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1}),"th of every layer. FSDP2 gathers and releases parameters by unit, a module whose parameters it treats together, and the diagram takes each layer as one unit. Its two lanes stand for any two ranks of the sharding group, and the lifecycle they draw comes from PyTorch FSDP ",e.jsx(a,{id:"zhao2023fsdp",children:"Zhao et al., 2023"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Before a layer's forward, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"GATHER WEIGHTS"})," all-gathers its full parameters from all ranks. After the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"FORWARD"}),", ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"RESHARD PARAMS"})," frees the gathered copy, so the rank keeps only its own shard again. The backward runs from the last layer to the first, and each layer's gradients need its weights, which were freed. So the backward gathers each layer's parameters a second time, computes its ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"BACKWARD"})," and reduce-scatters its gradients over the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"SYNC GRADS"})," connector. Each rank then holds the summed gradient of its own shard next to that shard's optimizer state, so the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"UPDATE"})," needs no communication. Unlike ZeRO-1, FSDP2 runs no all-gather after the update: the next forward gathers every layer anyway and picks up the updated shards then."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Why full sharding sends 1.5 times DDP's bytes"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Count with the ideal ring at ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N = 4",display:!1,trail:"."})," One reduce-scatter or all-gather over the whole model sends ",e.jsx(s,{math:"P(N-1)/N = 0.75P",display:!1})," per rank. DDP and ZeRO-1 each run two such passes, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"1.5P",display:!1})," per rank. Full sharding runs three: the forward gathers, the backward gathers and the gradient reduce-scatters each send ",e.jsx(s,{math:"0.75P",display:!1,trail:"."})," That is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2.25P",display:!1})," per rank, the end value of the stepper's ZeRO-3 mode. The ratio to DDP is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2.25P/(1.5P) = 1.5",display:!1,trail:"."})," The extra half is the second all-gather of the parameters, for the backward pass."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(s,{math:"1.5\\times",display:!1})," compares sent volume. Prefetching a layer's parameters while another layer computes can hide part of the traffic ",e.jsx(a,{id:"zhao2023fsdp",children:"Zhao et al., 2023"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Each ZeRO stage shards one more part",accent:5,number:"22",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Sharded"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Collectives per step"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Communication, × DDP"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"State per GPU, 7B on 4 GPUs"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"DDP"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"nothing"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"all-reduce gradients"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.0×"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"112 GB"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"ZeRO-1"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"optimizer state"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"reduce-scatter gradients, all-gather weights"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.0×"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"49 GB"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"ZeRO-2"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"+ gradients"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"as ZeRO-1"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.0×"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"38.5 GB"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"ZeRO-3"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"+ weights"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"two all-gathers of weights, one reduce-scatter"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.5×"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"28 GB"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Only full sharding costs extra communication",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Each memory figure divides the sharded parts of the 16 B by four and keeps the rest whole. ZeRO-1 gives ",e.jsx(s,{math:"(2 + 2 + 12/4)\\,\\text{B} \\times 7 \\times 10^9 = 49\\,\\text{GB}",display:!1})," per GPU. Under ZeRO-2 each rank keeps only its reduce-scatter output, its shard of the reduced gradient, and frees the gradients of the other shards. That gives ",e.jsx(s,{math:"(2 + 2/4 + 12/4)\\,\\text{B} \\times 7 \\times 10^9 = 38.5\\,\\text{GB}",display:!1})," per GPU, while the full parameters stay resident through forward and backward. ZeRO-3 shards all 16 B, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"16\\,\\text{B} / 4 \\times 7 \\times 10^9 = 28\\,\\text{GB}",display:!1})," per GPU. The figures count the resident training state alone."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"So the first two stages save memory for no extra bytes. Only full sharding trades memory for communication, and the price is the second gather of every layer."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"On four GPUs, ZeRO-1 fits 7B",accent:6,number:"23",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
