`]}),e.jsx(m,{strategy:"tp"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Column then row keeps GELU local and needs one all-reduce each way",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["ZeRO shards the storage of the training state, but every GPU still runs every layer on the full activations of its own slice of the batch. That is not enough when the activations of a single layer, or of a single matrix multiplication, are too large for one GPU. Tensor parallelism (TP) splits the weight matrices of each layer across GPUs, and each GPU computes its own part of the layer. The idea goes back to the intra-layer model parallelism of Megatron-LM ",e.jsx(a,{id:"shoeybi2019megatron",children:"Shoeybi et al., 2019"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The MLP of Week 2 shows how the split works. Its input is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"X",display:!1})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,D]"}),", with batch size ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B",display:!1,trail:","})," sequence length ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S",display:!1})," and model width ",e.jsx(s,{math:"D",display:!1,trail:"."})," Here ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S",display:!1})," is no longer the byte count of formula (1). The MLP computes ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\operatorname{GELU}(XW_1)\\,W_2",display:!1})," with ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_1",display:!1})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[D,d_ff]"})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_2",display:!1})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[d_ff,D]"}),", where ",e.jsx(s,{math:"d_{ff}",display:!1})," is the MLP width. Let ",e.jsx(s,{math:"t",display:!1})," be the TP degree, the number of GPUs that share each layer. Both matrices are split along the same ",e.jsx(s,{math:"d_{ff}",display:!1})," coordinates, so ",e.jsx(s,{math:"d_{ff}",display:!1})," must be divisible by ",e.jsx(s,{math:"t",display:!1,trail:"."})]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"The forward pass"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Rank ",e.jsx(s,{math:"r",display:!1})," holds a block of columns of the up-projection, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_1^{(r)}",display:!1})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[D,d_ff/t]"}),", and computes ",e.jsx(s,{math:"XW_1^{(r)}",display:!1})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,d_ff/t]"}),". That is a complete slice of the hidden activation. GELU acts on each element separately, so it applies to the slice with no communication. The down-projection is split by rows to match. Rank ",e.jsx(s,{math:"r",display:!1})," holds ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_2^{(r)}",display:!1})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[d_ff/t,D]"}),", the rows that multiply its hidden slice. Its product ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\operatorname{GELU}(XW_1^{(r)})\\,W_2^{(r)}",display:!1})," has the shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,D]"})," of the full output but is only a partial sum, because the MLP output ",e.jsx(s,{math:"Z",display:!1})," is the sum of the ",e.jsx(s,{math:"t",display:!1})," partial outputs. One all-reduce over the TP group adds them and leaves the full ",e.jsx(s,{math:"Z",display:!1})," on every rank."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"The backward pass"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The backward pass runs the same chain in reverse. The forward all-reduce left the same ",e.jsx(s,{math:"Z",display:!1})," on every rank, so every rank starts from the same output gradient ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\partial L/\\partial Z",display:!1})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,D]"}),". Write ",e.jsx(s,{math:"H^{(r)} = \\operatorname{GELU}(XW_1^{(r)})",display:!1})," for rank ",e.jsx(s,{math:"r",display:!1}),"'s hidden slice and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"A^{(r)} = XW_1^{(r)}",display:!1})," for the pre-activation slice under it."]}),e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Multiplying ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\partial L/\\partial Z",display:!1})," by the transpose of the row slice gives the gradient of the hidden slice, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\partial L/\\partial H^{(r)} = (\\partial L/\\partial Z)\\,W_2^{(r)\\top}",display:!1,trail:","})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,d_ff/t]"}),". The same two tensors give the gradient of the rank's own weights, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\partial L/\\partial W_2^{(r)} = H^{(r)\\top}\\,(\\partial L/\\partial Z)",display:!1,trail:"."})," Neither needs communication."]}),`
