`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Four activation all-reduces per layer decide where TP runs",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Each of these all-reduces carries a full ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,D]"})," tensor, batch by sequence by model width: the activations in the forward pass, their gradients in the backward pass. The next operation needs the summed result, so little computation is left to run alongside the all-reduce. That is what being on the critical path means. DDP's buckets hide its gradient all-reduce behind the backward pass of earlier layers, but TP's all-reduces add their time to every layer."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Their size and frequency make TP prefer the fastest link domain of the cluster, usually the NVLink inside one node. In Megatron-LM's experiments, throughput peaked when the TP degree equalled the eight GPUs of a node ",e.jsx(a,{id:"narayanan2021megatron",children:"Narayanan et al., 2021"}),". The actual placement still depends on the tensor sizes, on ",e.jsx(s,{math:"t",display:!1,trail:","})," on contention and on the link hierarchy."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["TP shards the attention and MLP blocks, but not every activation. The residual stream and the normalization and dropout between the blocks still work on full ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,D]"})," tensors, replicated on every rank of the TP group."]})]})]})]}),`
