`]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"One batch at a time keeps one stage busy",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Splitting the model by depth creates idle time, and the schedule, the order in which the stages run their forward and backward work, decides how much. Let ",e.jsx(s,{math:"p",display:!1})," be the number of pipeline stages. The schedules of this tab count time in units: every forward cell ",e.jsx(s,{math:"F",display:!1})," and every backward cell ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B",display:!1})," of a stage takes one unit, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"F = B = 1",display:!1,trail:"."})," Here ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B",display:!1})," is a backward cell, not the batch size of the Tensor Parallelism tab."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The naive schedule passes the whole batch through the stages one after another. Stage 0 runs its forward, then stage 1, and so on to the last stage, and the backward runs the same path in reverse. At any moment exactly one stage computes while the others wait. A step takes ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2p",display:!1})," time units, and each stage is busy for 2 of them, one forward and one backward. Each stage is therefore idle for ",e.jsx(s,{math:"(2p-2)/(2p) = (p-1)/p",display:!1})," of the step, and that share grows with the number of stages. Four GPUs then deliver the throughput of about one."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the explorer, each row is a stage, S0 at the top, and each column one time unit. A cell ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"F0"})," or ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"B0"})," is the forward or backward of the batch at that stage, and dim cells are idle."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Microbatches shrink the bubble",accent:0,number:"30",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
