`]}),e.jsx(y,{}),e.jsx(s,{math:"\\text{bubble fraction} = \\frac{p-1}{m+p-1}",label:"(2)"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"GPipe and 1F1B share a 3/11 bubble, and 1F1B needs less memory",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A pipeline keeps its stages busy by cutting the batch into ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m",display:!1})," ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"microbatches"}),", smaller groups of examples that pass through the stages one after another. While stage 1 runs the forward of the first microbatch, stage 0 already starts the second, so several stages work at once. The idle time that remains while the pipeline fills and drains is the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"bubble"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["GPipe pipelines the microbatches this way and finishes all the forwards before the backward wave starts from the last stage ",e.jsx(a,{id:"huang2019gpipe",children:"Huang et al., 2019"}),". Microbatch ",e.jsx(s,{math:"i",display:!1,trail:","})," counted from 0, reaches stage ",e.jsx(s,{math:"s",display:!1})," at time ",e.jsx(s,{math:"s + i",display:!1,trail:"."})," The forward wave therefore ends after ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m + p - 1",display:!1})," units, when the last microbatch leaves the last stage. The backward wave runs the same path in reverse and takes another ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m + p - 1",display:!1})," units. Of the step's ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2(m + p - 1)",display:!1})," units, each stage is busy for ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2m",display:!1,trail:","})," one forward and one backward per microbatch. Its remaining ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2(p - 1)",display:!1})," units are idle, where each wave fills and drains. Dividing these idle units by the length of the step gives formula (2):"]}),e.jsx(s,{math:"\\text{bubble fraction} = \\frac{2(p-1)}{2(m+p-1)} = \\frac{p-1}{m+p-1}."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["For the slide's case that is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"3/11 \\approx 27.27\\%",display:!1,trail:","})," down from three quarters in the naive pipeline."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The bubble shrinks as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m",display:!1})," grows, because filling and draining cost ",e.jsx(s,{math:"p - 1",display:!1})," units each, however many microbatches follow. GPipe's memory grows with ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m",display:!1,trail:","})," though. A stage keeps a microbatch's activations from its forward until its backward, because the backward needs them. Stage 0 runs all ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m",display:!1})," forwards before the first backward reaches it, so at its fullest it holds the activations of all ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m",display:!1})," microbatches."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["1F1B reorders the same work ",e.jsx(a,{id:"narayanan2019pipedream",children:"Narayanan et al., 2019"}),". Stage 0 runs a warmup of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"p",display:!1})," forwards until the backward of its first microbatch returns. From then on, each stage alternates one forward with one backward. Each backward releases one microbatch before the next forward adds one, so stage 0 never holds more than ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\min(p, m)",display:!1})," microbatches: 4 here, against GPipe's 8. In the unit-time model the 1F1B step is as long as GPipe's, so its bubble is the same ",e.jsx(s,{math:"3/11",display:!1,trail:"."})," 1F1B only reorders the work between the fill and the drain: the first backward still reaches stage 0 after ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2p - 1",display:!1})," units, and each stage still does ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2m",display:!1})," units of work. The same bubble with memory for ",e.jsx(s,{math:"p",display:!1})," microbatches instead of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m",display:!1})," makes 1F1B the baseline schedule."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the explorer, the bubble is the dim triangles at the start and the end of each wave. The in-flight readout counts the microbatches whose activations stage 0 holds at its peak, a proxy for activation memory. Raise ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m",display:!1,trail:":"})," the bubble shrinks under both schedules, and only GPipe's peak grows."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The unit-time model leaves out unequal stages, handoff latency and overlap. Its ",e.jsx(s,{math:"F = B",display:!1})," only gives the schedule cells equal width. The ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B \\approx 2F",display:!1})," of Week 2's ",e.jsx(s,{math:"6NT",display:!1})," count is a different statement: it compares the total forward and backward compute of a step."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["More microbatches also mean a larger batch. Let ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b_{\\mu}",display:!1})," be the number of examples in one microbatch. With ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b_{\\mu}",display:!1})," fixed, every extra microbatch adds ",e.jsx(s,{math:"b_{\\mu}",display:!1})," examples to a step, so a pipeline pushes a run toward larger global batches. Week 5's critical batch size limits that trade. Past it, a larger batch no longer cuts the number of steps a run needs, so a smaller bubble bought with a larger batch stops paying for itself."]})]})]}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"1F1B at p = 4, m = 8: the bubble stays at 27.3%, while stage 0 holds at most 4 microbatches instead of GPipe's 8.",children:e.jsx(y,{initialSchedule:"1f1b"})})]}),`
