`]}),e.jsx(u,{}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Keep the block inputs, recompute the inside",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Training keeps activations because the backward pass needs them. The gradient of a layer's weights is computed from the input that layer saw in the forward pass. Every activation the forward pass produces must therefore either stay in memory until the backward pass reaches it or be computed again. The ZeRO stages shard the training state and leave activations untouched, and tensor and pipeline parallelism split only some of them. So activations still press on memory after the training state is sharded, and they grow with the batch size and the sequence length."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Activation checkpointing keeps only some activations and computes the rest again when the backward pass needs them ",e.jsx(a,{id:"chen2016checkpointing",children:"Chen et al., 2016"}),". The usual choice of what to keep is the input of each transformer block, called its checkpoint. The forward pass frees the activations inside a block as soon as the block's output exists. When the backward pass reaches a block, it first reruns the block's forward from the stored input. That rematerializes the interior activations for the block's backward pass, and they are freed again before the backward pass moves on to the next block."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the stepper, run one training step with checkpointing off, then on. With it off, the forward pass keeps all four activations, and the backward pass uses and frees them from right to left. With it on, the forward pass keeps ",e.jsx(s,{math:"X_1",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"X_2",display:!1})," and frees ",e.jsx(s,{math:"R_1",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"R_2",display:!1})," as soon as the next node exists. The backward pass first recomputes ",e.jsx(s,{math:"R_2",display:!1})," from ",e.jsx(s,{math:"X_2",display:!1})," and sends the gradient through block 2, then recomputes ",e.jsx(s,{math:"R_1",display:!1})," from ",e.jsx(s,{math:"X_1",display:!1})," and sends it through block 1. The readouts show the trade. Without checkpointing, the forward pass computes each of the five nodes from ",e.jsx(s,{math:"X_1",display:!1})," to the loss once, and at most four activations are held at once. With it, the two recomputations raise the forward computations to seven, and the peak drops to three."]})]})]}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"After the forward pass only the checkpoints X₁ and X₂ are held, and R₁ and R₂ were freed.",children:e.jsx(u,{initialPhase:5})}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"The backward pass recomputed R₂ and R₁ from their checkpoints, so the peak was 3 activations instead of 4, for 2 extra forward computations.",children:e.jsx(u,{initialPhase:9})}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Checkpointing trades compute for activation memory",reading:!0,accent:2,number:"32",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
